Yes, the issue in this code is that the `Sort()` method of the List class in C# does not return a sorted list, but instead sorts the original list in place and returns void. Therefore, you cannot assign the result of `Sort()` to a new list as shown in the code above.
To fix this issue, you can simply remove the assignment of `sortedNums` and instead call `Sort()` directly on `nums`:
```
List<int> nums = new List<int>(){3, 1, 4, 2, 5};
nums.Sort();
foreach (int num in nums)
{
   Console.WriteLine(num);
}
```
This will sort the `nums` list in place and then print out the sorted values without any errors.

